SCOPE CHECKED: internal/services/storage/storage.go (per .swarm/manifests/R11.2.files)
plus a repo-wide grep for cross-cutting dependencies on the old staging name,
per the brief's own precedent-reuse instruction and the checker task's
explicit prompt to check for exactly this.

## Storage.go itself: correct on every axis tested

atomicWrite now does CreateTemp(dir, base+".tmp-*") -> Write -> Close -> Chmod
-> Rename, with deferred os.Remove(tmpPath) as a backstop. I verified, by
building and running targeted Go tests against the real code (not just
reading it):

- Reader's view: ReadFile stats/reads only `path`, never globs the directory,
  so it can only observe the pre-write file or the fully-written post-rename
  file, never the staging file or a torn destination. Confirmed by tracing
  ReadFileContext (storage.go:226) — no directory listing, no glob.
- Permissions: 0600/0644/0666 round-trip exactly under umask 0022
  (TestZZAdHocPermsAcrossValues, PASS). Chmod operates on path, not fd, so
  umask at CreateTemp time (which forces 0600) does not leak through — the
  explicit Chmod always wins. Rewriting over an existing destination with a
  different perm also lands the newly requested perm exactly
  (TestZZAdHocPermsOverwriteDifferentPerm, PASS).
- Cross-filesystem / directory derivation: filepath.Dir is used for both
  MkdirAll and CreateTemp, including the no-directory-component case
  ("bare.txt" -> dir "."); staging and destination share a directory in every
  case tested (TestZZAdHocNoDirComponent, PASS).
- Failure paths: forced Rename failure (destination pre-exists as a
  directory) returns a non-swallowed error ("file exists") and leaves zero
  staging files behind (TestZZAdHocCleanupOnRenameFailure, PASS). Forced
  CreateTemp failure (read-only destination directory) returns a
  non-swallowed "permission denied" error and leaves zero staging files
  behind (TestZZAdHocReadOnlyDirCreateTempFails, PASS).
- Cleanup race: CreateTemp's uniqueness guarantee holds; the deferred Remove
  targets a name no other writer can produce, and it no-ops on the success
  path (target already moved away by Rename). Reasoning verified, not just
  read.
- Encryption: full existing encryption suite (round-trip, marker-file skip,
  locked/unlocked, age + SSH providers) passes under -race including through
  atomicWrite.
- Out-of-scope sites: `git diff HEAD -- migration.go auth.go` is empty —
  migration.go:247, migration.go:275, auth.go:92 are untouched, confirmed by
  diff not by inspection.
- Subtler-mutation probe of the ORACLE (not the delivered code): I mutated a
  scratch copy of atomicWrite three ways and re-ran accept.sh's oracle
  (TestZZOracleR11*):
    (a) drop the deferred cleanup entirely -> oracle still exits 0. The
        oracle's "no staging file survives" check (Check 2) only exercises
        the success path, where Rename already removes the tmp file
        regardless of the defer; it never forces a write to fail. My own
        forced-failure test (above) DOES catch this mutation (FAIL, staging
        file leaked). So the oracle has a real coverage gap on exactly the
        brief's third scope bullet ("clean up the staging file if the write
        fails") — it happens not to matter here because the delivered code
        does have the defer, confirmed independently.
    (b) Chmod before Close, (c) Rename before Close/Close-after-Rename: both
        pass the oracle and are not actually behavioral bugs on Linux (Chmod
        operates on the path, not the fd; Rename of an open fd's path is
        legal POSIX). Not a finding.
  This is reported as an oracle-robustness gap, not counted against the
  verdict on its own, because the delivered code is independently correct on
  the failure-cleanup requirement.

## The regression that drives the FAIL verdict

Per the checker task's explicit instruction to grep the repo for anything
depending on the OLD ".tmp" staging name (cleanup routine / glob / backup
exclusion / gitignore):

`internal/services/backup/snapshot.go`:
  const tmpSuffix = ".tmp"
  // SkipPredicate ... atomicWrite *.tmp leftovers ...
  func SkipPredicate(...) func(path string, isDir bool) bool {
      ...
      if strings.HasSuffix(base, tmpSuffix) { return true }   // line ~267
      ...
  }

SkipPredicate's documented job (its own doc comment: "Skip atomicWrite
leftovers and encryption-state files") is to exclude a crash-orphaned
atomicWrite staging file from (1) backup zip contents (filepath.Walk over
DataDir in buildZip, snapshot.go:167-208) and (2) restore-time pruning
protection (restore.go:119, "a file present on disk but absent from the
archive is removed unless the skip predicate protects it").

The OLD staging name (`path + ".tmp"`) has suffix ".tmp" and was correctly
matched. The NEW staging name, from
`os.CreateTemp(dir, filepath.Base(path)+".tmp-*")`, is `<base>.tmp-<8 random
digits>` — e.g. `sidecar.json.tmp-2496562633` — which does NOT have suffix
".tmp" (it has ".tmp-" followed by digits). I confirmed this breaks the
predicate with a targeted test against the real SkipPredicate function:

  TestZZAdHocSkipPredicateNewStagingName:
    oldStyle := "/data/sidecar.json.tmp"          -> skip() = true  (as before)
    newStyle := "/data/sidecar.json.tmp-2496562633" -> skip() = false (BROKEN)
  --- FAIL: NEW-style staging name NOT skipped by SkipPredicate

Consequence: if a process is killed between os.CreateTemp and the deferred
Remove/Rename in atomicWrite (the exact scenario cleanOrphanTmp's sibling
mechanism exists to guard against, and the exact scenario SkipPredicate's own
doc comment names), the orphaned staging file in DataDir will now be walked
into the next backup zip (previously excluded) and, on a later restore, will
NOT be treated as protected — it round-trips into backups and back onto disk
as permanent cruft instead of being excluded. This is a live behavioral
regression caused by R11's staging-name change, not speculative: reproduced
directly against the shipped SkipPredicate function, not against a
hypothetical.

The existing test `TestSkipPredicate_FilesUnderSkipListedDirs`
(snapshot_test.go) gives false confidence here: it hardcodes a literal
old-style name `"foo.tmp"` rather than deriving it from what atomicWrite
actually produces, so it keeps passing after R11 while the real
cross-package contract silently breaks. Neither the R11 oracle
(scoped to internal/services/storage only) nor the full test suite run in
report.md exercises this path, because no test constructs a live
`<base>.tmp-<rand>` file and walks it through SkipPredicate.

There is no other DataDir-scoped cleanup for orphaned atomicWrite staging
files (cleanOrphanTmp only sweeps BackupDir's own zip .tmp files, a different
mechanism) — SkipPredicate's suffix check was the only protection, and R11's
implementation broke it without touching or being told about
internal/services/backup/snapshot.go.

## Why this is R11's to answer, not out of scope

R11's brief explicitly chose the `.tmp-*` naming convention (mirroring
CreateExclusive) and explicitly enumerated three out-of-scope call sites by
name (migration.go:247/275, auth.go:92) that use the OLD fixed-name pattern
directly — but never audited or named backup/snapshot.go's SkipPredicate,
which does not call atomicWrite at all but encodes an assumption about its
output format. The SHARED-TREE CONTEXT note in the dispatch says R12/ledger
failures are not R11's to own; this is different — it is a direct, causal
consequence of the exact code change under review, in a critical-glob path
(internal/services/storage/**) that "every sidecar in the app writes
through," feeding a backup/restore path for a money-data application.

## Cleanup

All scratch test files (zz_failtest_test.go, zz_perm_test.go, zz_dir_test.go,
zz_fail2_test.go, zz_skippred_test.go) were created under
internal/services/storage/ and internal/services/backup/ during this
adversarial run and have been removed; storage.go was diffed byte-identical
back to its pre-experiment (post-R11-fix) state before finishing. `git status
--porcelain` on internal/services/storage and internal/services/backup shows
only the pre-existing staged R11 change to storage.go.

## FINDINGS SUMMARY

1. FAIL-worthy: internal/services/backup/snapshot.go's SkipPredicate silently
   stops recognizing atomicWrite's own staging files after this change,
   defeating its documented backup-exclusion / restore-pruning purpose for
   crash-orphaned writes. Reproduced with a direct test against the real
   function.
2. Non-blocking process note: the Tier-3 oracle's "no staging file survives"
   check only exercises the success path and would not have caught a dropped
   deferred-cleanup; the delivered code is independently correct there, so
   this alone does not change the verdict, but the oracle should not be
   trusted as proof of the brief's explicit failure-path-cleanup requirement.
3. Everything else attacked (reader atomicity, permissions incl. umask,
   directory derivation incl. no-dir/relative paths, failure-path error
   propagation, cleanup-race uniqueness, encryption round-trip, out-of-scope
   sites) held up under direct testing, not just inspection.
